@Tutorials(name: "XitDB") {
    @Intro(title: "Learning XitDB") {
        Work through a database from its first commit: keep its history, build an index that it does not give you, and
        keep a copy of it up to date. Each tutorial is one program that grows step by step. The package's tests compile and
        run the finished program, which holds the code of every step.
    }

    @Chapter(name: "A database and its history") {
        Create a database in a file, read it back, and use what an immutable database is for: going back to any earlier
        state of it.

        @Image(source: "history-on-dark.svg", alt: "A history of three moments, each a map. The later moments share with the earlier ones the values they did not change.")

        @TutorialReference(tutorial: "doc:YourFirstDatabase")
        @TutorialReference(tutorial: "doc:KeepingHistory")
    }

    @Chapter(name: "Building on it") {
        Two things a database like this leaves to you: an index, and a copy kept up to date.

        @Image(source: "replica-on-dark.svg", alt: "A source and its replica, side by side. The replica holds the same bytes as the start of the source, and the bytes it lacks are copied across.")

        @TutorialReference(tutorial: "doc:AnIndexOfYourOwn")
        @TutorialReference(tutorial: "doc:KeepingAReplicaInStep")
    }
}
